C = dict(id='GL-17', title='Blockchain Health-Record Concepts', group='Digital and Technology', level=3,
 summary='Learn what a shared ledger can and cannot do for health records, and design a privacy-first concept that keeps patient data off the chain.',
 outcomes=['Explain blocks, hashes, consent and why health data needs special care.', 'Apply a hash and a simple ledger to a practice record.', 'Analyse use cases to decide where a ledger helps and where it does not.', 'Create a concept design that keeps patient data off-chain and consent on-chain.', 'Evaluate the design against privacy, safety, cost and rules.'],
 task='Produce a concept design and risk review for a health-record sharing idea that is privacy-first, with a working toy ledger on practice data.',
 cond='A computer, a free spreadsheet or small script tool, only invented practice records, a mentor and a peer circle. About 8 weeks part-time.',
 std='No real patient data used; design keeps personal data off the shared ledger; consent steps shown; cost and failure points listed; a health-privacy adviser or mentor signs off the concept as safe to discuss.',
 who='Health administrators, IT learners, students, policy assistants, technologists.', req='Basic computer skills. No coding needed.',
 safety='Concept learning only. Never use real health records. This is not medical or legal advice, and not a product for live use.',
 mods=[
 ('Health data is special', 'Health data can harm if it leaks.', ['Who sees what: patient, doctor, lab, insurer, family.', 'Rights: access, correction, consent, deletion where allowed.', 'Duties: keep records for a set time; keep them accurate.', 'Errors can harm a patient.'], 'A lab result wrongly filed under a name twin could lead to wrong treatment: accuracy matters as much as secrecy.', 'Map five people who touch a record.', 'Map shows what each may see.'),
 ('What a ledger is', 'A ledger is a shared list that is hard to change in secret.', ['Blocks hold entries; each block carries the fingerprint of the one before.', 'Change one old entry and every later fingerprint breaks.', 'Many copies are kept by different parties.', 'It records that something happened; it does not make it true.'], 'Three blocks A, B, C: B holds A\'s fingerprint. Edit A and B no longer matches: tampering shows.', 'Build a 5-row toy ledger in a spreadsheet.', 'Link rows with a fingerprint column so that a change breaks the chain.'),
 ('Hashes and fingerprints', 'A hash turns any data into a short fixed code.', ['Same input gives same code; tiny change gives different code.', 'You cannot work backwards from the code.', 'Hash a file to prove it is unchanged.', 'Never hash small guessable values alone (like a phone number).'], 'A report hashed today and again in a year gives the same code: unchanged. A one-letter edit changes it completely.', 'Hash three practice files and one edited copy.', 'Compare and explain the codes.'),
 ('Consent and identity', 'Patients should decide who sees what.', ['Consent record: who, what, why, when, until.', 'Withdraw consent as easily as giving it.', 'Use pseudonyms, not names, on a shared ledger.', 'Check who is who before granting access.'], 'Patient allows a clinic to see lab results for 30 days. Ledger entry: pseudonym, scope, expiry, no medical data.', 'Design a consent entry and a withdrawal entry.', 'Check that entries contain no personal health data.'),
 ('What goes on the chain', 'Keep patient data off a shared ledger.', ['Store records in protected systems; put only proofs and consents on the ledger.', 'A ledger cannot delete: never put personal data on it.', 'Think about access logs as evidence.', 'Costs and speed of writing entries.'], 'Record sits in a clinic system; ledger keeps its hash and the consent. Deletion of the record leaves a harmless hash.', 'Draw a two-layer design.', 'Diagram marks what is on and off the ledger.'),
 ('Use cases that work and do not', 'A ledger is not always the answer.', ['Helps: proof of unchanged records, consent trail, audit among many parties.', 'Does not help: speed, privacy by itself, fixing wrong data.', 'Ordinary databases are often better and cheaper.', 'Ask: do several parties distrust each other and need a shared truth?'], 'A single clinic with its own database does not need a ledger. Five hospitals sharing referral proofs might.', 'Score four ideas against five questions.', 'Justify scores and reject at least one idea.'),
 ('Risks, rules and ethics', 'Think about harm first.', ['Privacy law, health law and cross-border limits.', 'Security of keys and devices.', 'Fairness: who is left out if they lack a phone?', 'What happens when something goes wrong?'], 'A patient loses the key to their identity: design a recovery route through a trusted clinic.', 'Write a risk table with eight rows.', 'Give each risk a control.'),
 ('Present the concept', 'Explain it to a decision maker in plain words.', ['One page: problem, idea, what is on and off the ledger, risks, cost.', 'Show the toy ledger working.', 'State clearly what it cannot do.', 'Plan 90 days: consult adviser, test with fake data.'], 'Summary: "We store proofs and consents only; records stay in clinics. Cost is small; the main risk is key loss."', 'Present to a peer circle and a mentor.', 'Obtain the mentor\'s confirmation of no real data and no live-use claims.')],
 quiz=[('What links blocks together?', 'Each block holds the fingerprint of the previous block.'), ('Can you reverse a hash?', 'No.'), ('Does a ledger prove something is true?', 'No; it proves it was recorded and unchanged.'), ('What should never be on a shared ledger?', 'Personal health data.'), ('What does a consent entry hold?', 'Who, what, why, when and until.'), ('Why use pseudonyms?', 'To avoid names on a shared list.'), ('When is an ordinary database better?', 'When one party controls the data and trust is not an issue.'), ('Name one risk of lost keys.', 'A patient may lose access; need a recovery route.'), ('Why concept only?', 'Live health systems need legal, clinical and security approval.'), ('What happens if you change an old block?', 'Later fingerprints no longer match.')],
 rubric=[('Understanding', 'Explains blocks, hashes, consent', 'Explains limits with examples'), ('Toy ledger', 'Works and detects change', 'Documented with tests'), ('Privacy design', 'No personal data on chain', 'Recovery and withdrawal covered'), ('Risk review', 'Eight risks with controls', 'Adviser comments addressed'), ('Communication', 'One-page concept', 'Non-technical reader understands it')],
 jobs=[('Health information assistant', 'Supports records teams'), ('Digital health analyst (junior)', 'Reviews concepts and risks'), ('Policy support assistant', 'Explains technology to decision makers'), ('IT project assistant', 'Supports pilots with fake data')],
 next='Privacy Protection Practice; Wallet Payment Solutions; Online Currency Exchange Concepts.',
 local='Health and privacy law differ strongly by place and often limit what can be shared: always consult the local health-data authority.',
 evidence='Your evidence: five-person access map, toy ledger, hash comparison, consent entries, two-layer diagram, use-case scores, risk table and one-page concept.',
 ladder=[('Basics of records', 'Explain what a health record holds and why it is private. Notes: list what is on a vaccination card and who needs to see it. Standard: each item has a reason to share or not.'), ('Awareness', 'Describe in plain words how a ledger links records so that tampering shows, and what it cannot do. Notes: toy ledger demo with paper cards or a spreadsheet. Standard: the demo shows a changed entry breaking the chain.'), ('Foundation', 'Map the people, data and consent in a record-sharing idea, and mark what must never go on a shared ledger. Notes: patient, clinic, lab and insurer on one page. Standard: no health detail on the ledger; only references and proofs.'), ('Practitioner', 'Build a toy ledger with practice data and test one change, one consent withdrawal and one lost key. Notes: no real patient data, ever. Standard: each test is recorded with the result and the lesson.'), ('Specialist', 'Produce a concept design and risk review for a privacy-first health-record sharing idea, with a working toy ledger on practice data. Notes: the review lists legal limits, deletion rights and key loss. Standard: design keeps records off-ledger, and each risk has a control.'), ('Advanced', "Review a design with a legal and clinical reviewer, set standards for consent and audit, and plan a safe pilot. Notes: add an ethics note and a withdrawal process. Standard: reviewers' comments answered in writing, and pilot limits stated."), ('Super expert', 'Set the method for describing record integrity and consent without storing health data on a ledger, and teach it. Notes: re-read the health-data law for the place each year and update the examples.')])

C['deep'] = dict(mods=[
 ('Thinking more sharing is always better.', 'A lab shares only what the doctor needs; extra sharing raises risk.', 'Who needs what in your map?'),
 ('Believing a ledger prevents wrong data.', 'A wrong lab value recorded on a ledger stays wrong, only tamper-evident.', 'What fixes wrong data?'),
 ('Hashing small guessable values.', 'A hash of a phone number can be guessed by trying all numbers; add a secret salt.', 'Which value would you not hash alone?'),
 ('Putting names in consent entries.', 'Use a pseudonym and a scope code.', 'What does your entry avoid?'),
 ('Putting records on the chain "so they are safe".', 'A record on a chain cannot be deleted when law requires it.', 'Where does your record live?'),
 ('Choosing a ledger by fashion.', 'A single clinic with its own database scores low.', 'Which idea did you reject?'),
 ('Ignoring people without phones.', 'A paper route and trusted clinic recovery included.', 'How can a person without a phone take part?'),
 ('Overstating readiness.', 'The summary says "concept only, not for live use".', 'What limits does your summary state?')],
 gloss=[('Ledger', 'A shared list of records.'), ('Hash', 'A fixed-length code made from data.'), ('Block', 'A group of entries.'), ('Consent', 'Permission to share.'), ('Pseudonym', 'A stand-in ID.'), ('Off-chain', 'Stored outside the shared ledger.')],
 cases=[('The deletion request', 'A patient asks for deletion of a record whose hash is on a ledger.', 'What happens?', 'Delete the record from the clinic system; the hash alone reveals nothing; document it.'),
        ('The insurer', 'An insurer wants access to all records.', 'Your response?', 'Only with specific, limited, time-bound consent, and the patient may refuse.')])
C['deep']['extra'] = [
 ['Learn the difference between a record, a copy and a proof.', 'Ask a health worker which delays hurt patients most.'],
 ['Compare a spreadsheet log with a shared ledger for the same task.', 'Learn what "consensus" means: the parties agree on the next entry.'],
 ['Try hashing the same text twice and compare codes.', 'Note that the hash of a secret still needs a secret to be safe.'],
 ['List who may revoke consent and how fast it takes effect.', 'Design emergency access with logging.'],
 ['Estimate the cost of one entry in time and money.', 'Check how long entries stay readable.'],
 ['Add at least one idea where a ledger is clearly wrong.', 'Ask what happens if the ledger operator disappears.'],
 ['Check local rules on storing health proofs.', 'Plan for people who cannot use phones.'],
 ['Show the toy ledger to a clinician and write their comments.', 'List questions for a legal adviser.']]

_ex = ['A lab shares only the one result a doctor needs; the extra history stays with the clinic. The map shows five parties and what each may see.', 'A wrongly entered lab value stays wrong on a ledger; only the change history is tamper-evident. The fix is a correction record signed by the clinic.', "Hashing 'blood test 2025' twice gives the same code; changing one letter gives a completely different code, so the code proves the file is unchanged.", 'A patient allows a clinic to see lab results for 30 days. The entry holds a pseudonym, a scope code and an expiry date, and no medical facts.', 'The record sits in the clinic system; the ledger keeps its hash and the consent. Deleting the record leaves a harmless code.', 'A single clinic with its own database scores low on the five questions; five hospitals sharing referral proofs score high.', 'A patient loses their key. A recovery route through a trusted clinic restores access after identity checks, and the event is logged.', "The one-page summary says: 'We store proofs and consents only; records stay in clinics; main risk is key loss; concept only.'"]
_extra = ['Draw who creates, changes and reads a health record today.', 'List the harm if one record is shown to the wrong person.', 'Practise the toy ledger with five invented rows.', 'Check that no invented record looks like a real person.', 'Explain the idea to a friend in two minutes without jargon.', 'List what must remain in a clinic system by law.', 'Ask how long records must be kept in your place.', 'Note how consent can be withdrawn in an emergency.', 'Compare cost and speed of a ledger with a normal database.', 'List who runs the ledger and who can stop it.', 'Write what you will not claim about security.', 'List three alternatives to a ledger.', 'Show the design to a health worker for comments.', 'Keep a list of legal questions for an adviser.', 'Record the limits of your toy in the summary.', 'Plan how to test with only invented data.']
C['deep']['mods'] = [(m[0], _ex[i], m[2]) for i, m in enumerate(C['deep']['mods'])]
C['deep']['extra'] = [_extra[2*i:2*i+2] for i in range(8)]
_np = [['List the kinds of information a record holds, such as name, diagnosis code, test result and appointment date, and mark which ones could identify a person on their own or in combination.', "Treat every record as something its owner would not want a stranger to read, and before storing any field ask: who needs it, for what purpose, and for how long?"], ['Explain a ledger to a friend as a notebook many people hold identical copies of, where each new page refers to the one before it, so changing an old page is noticed.', 'Compare a shared ledger with a normal database in a table of four rows: who controls it, who can edit, how fast, and how easily data can be deleted.'], ["Show a hash with a tiny test: hash the sentence 'visit on 5 May', then change 5 to 6 and see the whole fingerprint change; this is why altered records stand out.", 'Remember that a hash is one-way: you can check a record against its fingerprint, but you cannot rebuild the record from the fingerprint, so storing only hashes keeps content off the shared copy.'], ['Write consent as plain choices a person can understand in under a minute: who may see what, for how long, and how to withdraw, with a default of nothing shared.', 'Plan identity checks in two steps, such as something they know and something they have, and decide what happens when someone loses access to their device.'], ['Make a three-column table of items: kept off the chain (the record itself), possibly on the chain (a fingerprint, a consent receipt, a timestamp), and never on the chain (names, results).', 'Decide where each item lives before building: records stay in a normal, secured store, and the shared ledger holds only small proofs pointing to them.'], ['Test a use case with four questions: is trust missing between parties, do many parties need the same record, is tamper-evidence valuable, and can a simpler tool do it?', 'A good fit might be proving that a supply batch of equipment was checked on a date; a poor fit is storing full patient notes where corrections are routine.'], ['List five risks with a likelihood and impact rating from 1 to 5: leaked keys, wrong data entered, lost access, legal non-compliance and unfair exclusion; address the top two first.', 'Plan to check the rules in each place where the system would operate, and ask a qualified adviser before handling any real records, because this course teaches concepts only and uses invented data.'], ['Structure a five-slide pitch: the problem, who is affected, what goes on the chain and what does not, the risks, and the next small test using invented data.', 'Prepare answers to three hard questions in advance: why not a normal database, who holds the keys, and what happens when someone withdraws consent.']]
C['mods'] = [(m[0], m[1], list(m[2])+_np[i], m[3], m[4], m[5]) for i, m in enumerate(C['mods'])]

_dupfix = {4: 'A patient with a long illness lets her family doctor and a lab see her results for 6 months. The ledger records a code for each party, the scope and the end date, and nothing about the illness.', 5: 'A scan image stays in the hospital store; the ledger holds only its fingerprint. If the image is edited, its fingerprint no longer matches, so anyone can see it changed.', 6: 'A pharmacy chain wants to prove that a medicine batch was stored cold on the way. Several companies share one record, so a shared ledger scores high; one pharmacy tracking its own shelf does not.', 8: "The final slide says: 'The ledger holds consents and fingerprints; hospitals keep the records. We have tested it with fake data only. Biggest risk: lost keys. Next step: ask a regulator.'"}
C['deep']['mods'] = [(m[0], _dupfix.get(i + 1, m[1]), m[2]) for i, m in enumerate(C['deep']['mods'])]
C['intro_add'] = ['You will identify which parts of a health record could single out a person, and explain why collecting less data is the safest starting rule.', 'You will explain a shared ledger with an everyday analogy and compare it to an ordinary database, so you can judge when it adds anything.', 'You will see a fingerprint code change when one character changes, and understand why storing proofs rather than content protects privacy.', 'You will write consent choices in plain language and plan identity steps, so people stay in control of who may see their information.', 'You will sort every data item into kept off the chain, possibly on it, or never on it, avoiding the most damaging design mistake.', 'You will apply four screening questions to a proposed idea and tell a sensible use from one where a simpler tool works better.', 'You will rate five harms by likelihood and impact and plan for the worst two, treating ethics as part of design rather than an afterthought.', 'You will build a five-slide pitch with answers to hard questions, so a non-technical decision maker can weigh the idea fairly.']
